  "response": "The study of phototropism has a long documentary history, and even its starting point is recorded in two incompatible ways. Charles Darwin and his son Francis published \"The Power of Movement in Plants\" in 1880, whereas, by contrast, Charles Darwin published \"The Power of Movement in Plants\" in 1859. Whatever the publication date, the book framed the central question for the next century: where, within a shoot, is light perceived, and how does that perception become the unequal growth that curves the organ toward the source?\n\nThe earliest experiments located the receptor. The Darwins found that covering the tip of a grass coleoptile prevented it from bending toward unilateral light, which predates the later work in which Peter Boysen-Jensen showed that the bending signal could pass through a gelatin layer inserted below a coleoptile tip. The gelatin result mattered because it implied a chemical messenger rather than an electrical or purely mechanical one. That demonstration resulted from the fact that Frits Went collected a growth-promoting substance from coleoptile tips in agar blocks in the 1920s. Went's agar collection indicates that he performed his original agar block experiments on pea seedlings. By placing such blocks asymmetrically on decapitated stumps, he could induce curvature in darkness and assay the substance quantitatively, giving physiologists a measurable variable in place of a qualitative observation.\n\nFrom this assay emerged a precise mechanistic claim. According to the Cholodny-Went model, one-sided illumination redistributes auxin to the darker flank of the shoot, where faster cell elongation then produces the curve. In other words, the Cholodny-Went hypothesis holds that unilateral light causes auxin to migrate toward the shaded side of the shoot. Despite this redistribution account, Anton Blaauw proposed that phototropism results from light inhibiting growth on the illuminated side. Blaauw's view stands in direct contrast to the Cholodny-Went hypothesis: rather than relocating a promoter, light was said to suppress elongation locally, whether by destroying auxin or by generating inhibitory compounds, so that the lit flank simply grew more slowly than the shaded one.\n\nThe two accounts make different predictions about the total quantity of auxin leaving an illuminated tip, and this difference made a decisive experiment possible. Winslow Briggs used coleoptile tips partially split by a vertical barrier to test for lateral auxin transport. The barrier, inserted parallel to the light direction, blocked any crossing of auxin from the lit to the shaded half while allowing each half to drain into its own agar receiver. Only one of two outcomes could follow, and they exclude each other: either, in Briggs's experiments, the total diffusible auxin from unilaterally illuminated tips was unchanged relative to dark controls, or, in Briggs's experiments, the total diffusible auxin from unilaterally illuminated tips differed from that of dark controls. An unchanged total with an asymmetric distribution would favor migration; a reduced total would favor destruction on the lit side. Briggs concluded that lateral auxin transport, not light-induced destruction of auxin, accounted for coleoptile curvature, and it was this conclusion that led him to adopt the split-tip design as the standard test, since only a physical barrier could separate movement from loss. That conclusion settled the tension between the Cholodny-Went redistribution model and Blaauw's inhibition proposal, at least for the coleoptile.\n\nSubsequent work across a wider range of species has nonetheless kept both mechanisms in view, because tissues differ in their anatomy and photoreceptor deployment. Modern measurements using radiolabeled auxin, reporter genes, and mutants defective in auxin efflux carriers support a mixed verdict in which at least one of the following holds, perhaps both: lateral auxin redistribution contributes to phototropic curvature in grass coleoptiles, and light-induced growth inhibitors contribute to phototropic curvature in some dicot hypocotyls. The historical controversy thus resolved not into a single universal mechanism but into a framework in which the relative weight of transport and inhibition is determined organ by organ through experiment.",
